::ALG2_U01_L06_GP01::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L06_GP01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.7<br><br>Question:<br>Solve x<sup>3</sup> - 9x = 0. {
~x = -3 and x = 3 #This leaves out x = 0 from the factor x.
=x = -3, 0, 3 #Correct. Factor x(x squared - 9), then x(x - 3)(x + 3) = 0.
~x = 0 and x = 9 #The factor x squared - 9 gives plus or minus 3, not 9.
~x = -9, 0, 9 #These values come from the coefficient 9, not from solving x squared - 9 = 0.
}

::ALG2_U01_L06_GP02::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L06_GP02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.7<br><br>Question:<br>Solve x<sup>3</sup> - 4x<sup>2</sup> + x + 6 = 0, given that x = 2 is a solution. {
~x = 2 only #A cubic equation can have more than one solution.
=x = -1, 2, 3 #Correct. Dividing by x - 2 gives x squared - 2x - 3, which factors as (x - 3)(x + 1).
~x = -3, 1, 2 #These do not match the factors after division by x - 2.
~x = -2, 1, 3 #This set uses the wrong known factor.
}

::ALG2_U01_L06_GP03::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L06_GP03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.7<br><br>Question:<br>Solve x<sup>4</sup> - 16 = 0 over the complex number system. {
~x = -2 and x = 2 #These are only the real solutions and leave out the complex solutions.
=x = -2, 2, -2i, 2i #Correct. Factor as (x - 2)(x + 2)(x squared + 4), then solve each factor.
~x = -4, 4, -4i, 4i #These values come from 16 instead of the square root of 4.
~x = -2i and x = 2i #These are only the complex non-real solutions.
}

::ALG2_U01_L06_GP04::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L06_GP04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.7<br><br>Question:<br>Which solution has multiplicity 2 in (x - 5)<sup>2</sup>(x + 1) = 0? {
~x = -1 #This solution comes from x + 1, which appears once.
=x = 5 #Correct. The factor x - 5 appears twice, so x = 5 has multiplicity 2.
~x = -5 #The factor x - 5 gives x = 5, not -5.
~x = 1 #The factor x + 1 gives x = -1, not 1.
}

::ALG2_U01_L06_GP05::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L06_GP05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.7<br><br>Question:<br>Solve x<sup>3</sup> + x<sup>2</sup> + 4x + 4 = 0. {
~x = -1, -2, 2 #The quadratic factor is x squared plus 4, which gives complex solutions.
=x = -1, -2i, 2i #Correct. Factor by grouping to get (x + 1)(x squared + 4) = 0.
~x = 1, -2i, 2i #The linear factor x + 1 gives x = -1.
~x = -4 and x = 4 #These values do not solve the equation.
}
